*A Vignette about Chinatown’s Survival by Spectacle*

Chinatown’s architectural identity is often understood as evidence of cultural continuity: gateways, pagoda roofs, lanterns, ornamental balconies, Chinese signage, restaurants, and souvenir shops appear to preserve a piece of China within the American city. Yet Chinatown is not simply a reproduction of China. It is an American typology - an urban form produced through the particular conditions of immigration, racial exclusion, land ownership, and commerce in the United States. Its recognizable image was not simply inherited; it was deliberately constructed, in part, as a strategy for economic survival.

Chinatowns first emerged under conditions of exclusion, as Chinese immigrants concentrated geographically in response to racial discrimination and restrictions on where they could live, work, and participate in American society. The Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882 reinforced this isolation, turning Chinatown into both a refuge from the surrounding city and a neighborhood marked as fundamentally foreign.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-1.jpg" alt="Postcard of a street in San Francisco’s Chinatown, dated April 20, 1903, with a handwritten note: a most attractive place" /%}

By the early twentieth century, however, this perceived foreignness could be converted into something economically useful: attraction. The clearest example occurred after the 1906 San Francisco earthquake and fire destroyed much of Chinatown. City officials initially attempted to relocate the Chinese community away from its valuable downtown location. Chinese merchants resisted and instead proposed rebuilding an “Oriental City” that could attract tourists and strengthen Chinatown’s economic position.{% ref n=1 /%} Working with American architects, they reconstructed otherwise conventional American commercial buildings with curved eaves, pagoda towers, balconies, ornamental façades, and other motifs intended to communicate “Chineseness” to an American audience.{% ref n=2 /%} By 1908, Chinatown had effectively been reconstructed as an architectural spectacle.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-2.jpg" alt="Advertisement for the Sing Fat Co., “the famous Oriental bazaar,” a pagoda-topped building at California Street and Grant Avenue, Chinatown, San Francisco" /%}

This transformation changed Chinatown’s relationship to the surrounding city. Difference, once used to justify exclusion, became something that could be marketed. Restaurants, shops, festivals, architecture, and the streets themselves became part of an experience outsiders could consume. The SF Chinatown became a model for all the Chinatowns that followed in the United States. Tourism offered Chinese businesses revenue at a time when discrimination restricted access to many other parts of the economy. Chinatown therefore learned to make its cultural difference economically legible: to survive within the American city, Chinatown increasingly had to perform Chinatown.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-3.jpg" alt="Illustrated souvenir map of San Francisco’s Chinatown" pie="SF Chinatown Map from 1976" /%}

{% fuentes %}
1. On the post-earthquake relocation effort and Chinatown merchants' resistance, see Anh Sy Huy Le, “The ‘Orientals’ Strike Back: Displacement, Diasporic Resistance, and Spatial Justice in the 1906 San Francisco Earthquake and Fire,” Journal of Migration History (2018). Contemporary accounts show that city officials considered Chinatown's existing downtown land too valuable for continued Chinese occupation and proposed relocating the community to Hunters Point.
2. On the architectural construction of the rebuilt Chinatown, see “Generative Artifacts: Chinatown and an Ornamental Architecture of the Future,” Arts 14 (2025). The study describes the Sing Chong and Sing Fat buildings as examples of a deliberately constructed “pseudo-Oriental” architectural language intended to reposition Chinatown as a tourist destination. See also scholarship comparing pre- and post-1906 photographs, which identifies the rebuilt neighborhood as a fusion of Chinese motifs with American architectural elements, English-language signage, streetlights, and American flags.
{% /fuentes %}
